--TEST--
`net.local` is one grant over both ends of a socket path and reaches only the paths an operator wrote down — `rule:config/net-local-is-named-and-not-on-the-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// One grant governs both ends. A program that may create a socket at a path is
// a program that whatever else on this host finds it may speak to, so binding
// one is granted exactly as connecting to one is — and both doors below name
// the same capability when they are refused.
//
// What they are refused over is the path: this deployment granted one, and it
// is not the one the program asks for. A path-scoped grant reaches what an
// operator wrote and nothing beside it, which is why the refusal prints the
// path it was asked about rather than the grant's entry.
try {
    Core\Net::connectLocal("/run/novis/other.sock", 1s);
    echo "connected", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

try {
    Core\Net::listenLocal("/run/novis/other.sock");
    echo "bound", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
--FILE nvs.toml--
# One entry, and not the path the program asks for.
[capabilities.net]
local = ["/run/novis/app.sock"]
--EXPECT--
Core\Net::connectLocal needs the capability `net.local` for /run/novis/other.sock, which is not granted
Core\Net::listenLocal needs the capability `net.local` for /run/novis/other.sock, which is not granted
